  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations in New Mexico totaled $13.8B in FY2026 to date. The state recorded 8,134 awards, with an average award size of $1.7M, and obligations were up 2.4% year over year. The Department of Energy is the largest buyer, obligating $10.1B of the total. Facilities Support Services is the largest industry by obligations at $10B. The top vendors are TRIAD NATIONAL SECURITY, LLC with $5.2B and NATIONAL TECHNOLOGY & ENGINEERING SOLUTIONS OF SANDIA, LLC with $4.7B. Historical obligations show a steady rise from $11.6B in FY21 to $13.8B in FY26. Other major agencies in FY2026 to date include the Department of Homeland Security with $2B and the Department of Defense with $942M. Construction and research sectors also appear in the top industry list, led by Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $1.9B and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) at $272M. These figures reflect contract obligations for the fiscal year to date; current-year totals are partial.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who buys the most federal contracts in New Mexico?",
        "body": "The Department of Energy is the largest buying agency, obligating $10.1B in FY2026 to date. The Department of Homeland Security follows with $2B. The Department of Defense obligated $942M, and other buyers include the Department of the Interior with $323M and the Department of Agriculture with $182M. Several smaller agencies also contribute, including the Department of Health and Human Services at $166M and the Department of Veterans Affairs at $59.9M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which vendors and industries receive the largest awards?",
        "body": "TRIAD NATIONAL SECURITY, LLC leads vendors with $5.2B in obligations, followed by NATIONAL TECHNOLOGY & ENGINEERING SOLUTIONS OF SANDIA, LLC with $4.7B and FISHER SAND & GRAVEL CO with $1.8B. Facilities Support Services is the top industry at $10B, with Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $1.9B and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) at $272M. Other notable industry totals include Water and Sewer Line and Related Structures Construction at $227M and Engineering Services at $163M."
      }
    ]
